- `gallery-2.html`
- `gallery-3.html`
- `home-2.html`
- `home-3.html`
- `index-rtl.html`
- `index.html`
- `portfolio.html`
- `service-details.html`
- `service.html`
- `single-portfolio.html`
- `single-team.html`
- `single.html`
- `team.html`

- **Full pages** — e.g. `index.html`, `contact.html`, `service.html`: mirror full document structure when building a matching live page.
- **`element-*.html`** — partials and pattern libraries: use for sections (testimonials, CTA, team, pricing, etc.) when the main page does not include the variant you need.

## CSS and JavaScript

- **CSS** — Prefer `Template/css/styles.css` (and `Template/src` when present). Align `public/css/styles.css` selectors and structure with Template; keep one-off rules minimal.
- **JavaScript** — Prefer `Template/js/` (e.g. `custom-scripts.js`, carousel and plugin init). Keep `public/js` aligned with Template unless the project documents an intentional divergence.

## Workflow

1. Decide which **Template HTML** above matches the feature (full page or `element-*`).
2. Diff that file’s markup with `public/` (or Blade output) and match hierarchy, classes, and data attributes.
3. When behavior changes, check the same logic in `Template/js` / `Template/css` and mirror updates in `public/` where applicable.

## Scope

- Applies to static theme work in `public/` and any views that should mirror the theme.
- Backend-only changes (`routes`, `app/`, etc.) are outside this rule unless they emit HTML that should still follow these templates.
